@* The one choice between languages that their names do not settle for a teacher.
   A details element rather than a toggle in C#, so opening it is no round trip. *@

<details class="eal-note">
    <summary>Not sure which Chinese to choose?</summary>

    <p><strong>Simplified or Traditional?</strong></p>

    <p>
        Chinese is written in two ways, and our sheets come in both. Choose by where the
        pupil learned to read, not by the language they speak at home.
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>Chinese (Simplified):</strong> mainland China, Singapore and Malaysia</li>
        <li><strong>Chinese (Traditional):</strong> Hong Kong, Macau and Taiwan</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Mandarin and Cantonese are spoken forms of Chinese, so they don't settle this. A
        Cantonese speaker who went to school in Guangzhou will usually read Simplified,
        while one from Hong Kong will usually read Traditional.
    </p>

    <p>
        If you're not sure, ask the pupil or their family, or show them one sheet in each
        form. A pupil who reads one form can often make out the other, but it's slower
        and harder.
    </p>
</details>
